<article id="agents"><h2>What should change when an agent is wrong?</h2><p class="badge">Educational demonstration · research direction</p>
<h3>In everyday terms</h3><p>A route can look sensible until the world changes. The laboratory lets you close a passage and inspect what an agent observes, which part of its map it corrects, and why its next decision changes. You can move one step at a time, read the sequence in text, compare a revisable map with a frozen one, and download a record. This example makes the distinction between the world and a model of the world visible. It is a controlled illustration, with a map and rules supplied by the programmer; it does not demonstrate a trained neural agent or establish a general theory of intelligence.</p>
<h3>Technical approach and limitations</h3><p>The agent uses breadth-first search (BFS), a local cardinal sensor and an explicit map-revision policy. Compare policies while retaining the same fixture, transition rules and movement costs. Moves and logical ticks are different measurements. The protocol specifies interventions, stopping conditions and deterministic replay. Results describe the fixed scenario, not a population of independently sampled tasks. No learning benchmark, consciousness or biological mechanism is claimed.</p>
<p><a href="/projects/#agent-lab">Operate the laboratory</a> · <a href="/research/notes/demo-protocol.html">Read the protocol and source example</a> · <a href="https://github.com/Isaac-Daroch/Isaac-Daroch.github.io/tree/main/site/src/modules/agent-lab">Inspect the code</a></p>
<p>The separate <a href="/self-model/">self-model observatory</a> illustrates a bounded estimate of the agent’s own capabilities. It is distinct from this world-map experiment.</p></article>
<article id="clinical"><h2>Can natural language support governed clinical queries?</h2><p class="badge">Published article · August 2026</p>
<h3>In everyday terms</h3><p>The study examines an assistant for asking questions about outpatient schedules in Spanish. Turning a question into database instructions is only one part of the problem: the answer also needs to preserve the intended meaning and respect controlled access. The reported pilot distinguishes database-query correctness from answer correctness. This distinction helps explain why a syntactically valid query can still produce an unsuitable response.</p>
<h3>Technical summary and limitations</h3><p>The pilot combines a natural-language-to-SQL (Structured Query Language) interface with governed, read-only access to anonymized, delayed scheduling data. Evaluation uses audited interactions under institutional constraints. The evidence concerns this proof of concept; ambiguity, local terminology and faithful verbalization remain limitations. The website does not reproduce its clinical dataset or revalidate its experiments.</p>
<p><a href="/publications/">Read the article and bibliographic sources</a>. No public study repository or audiovisual demonstration has been confirmed in the supplied catalog.</p></article></div></BaseLayout>
